FQ.COMPANIONS = {
  tebrizi: {
    ic: "🧿", civ: "isl", zh: "星家帖必烈", en: "Tebrizi the Star-Reader",
    bioZh: "帖必烈——大不里士人叫他「骰子星家」，马拉盖旧人叫他「缺指的学徒」。他年轻时在纳西尔丁·图西手下磨镜、抄星表，左手食指被浑天仪铜环夹去一截，他称之为天文学的「血税」。蒙古兵火后，他从废墟扒出三本未尽的星表，驼皮裹好，至今压在床下，每晚翻一遍——星星会变慢，纸上的数字不会。观星台夜里他裹靛蓝旧袍读真星；白日在巴扎用三枚骨骰替码头苦力排「穷人也问得起」的天——一枚第纳尔教一夜真星，半枚只教骰子。他执意东行，不是为护送法兰克少年，而是想在有生之年亲眼看见东方星图与托勒密有何不同。他说法兰克人的天文学写得美，但美不等于准；准要靠象限仪，也要靠肯坐一夜的膝盖。途中他爱用枣与薄饼当干粮，拒茶视为失礼，拒星象劝告则被视为找死。若你肯载他一程，他会把丝囊里的骨骰交给你，并在戈壁的夜里指出织女那一点蓝：『骰子是捷径，蓝光才是师傅。』他睡觉也把骰子放在枕边——说梦里的星比醒时准，但只准给肯付一碗茶的人听。东行路上他很少问路人要钱——只问路人昨夜看见的星是什么颜色。",
    bioEn: "Tebrizi — 'the dice star-reader' in Tabriz, 'the fingerless apprentice' to old Maragha men. Young, he ground lenses and copied tables under Nasir al-Din al-Tusi; a brass armillary ring took the tip of his left index finger — the blood-tax of astronomy, he says. After Mongol fire he pulled three unburnt star-tables from the rubble, wrapped them in camel-leather, and still turns them every night under his bed — stars slow with age, numbers on paper never do. By night at the observatory he wraps an indigo robe and reads true stars; by day in the bazaar three bone dice serve dock-laborers a sky the poor can afford — one dinar for a night of real stars, half for dice alone. He insists on going east not to guard a Frankish youth, but to see with his own eyes how Cathay draws the sky, and whether it differs from Ptolemy. Frankish astronomy, he says, writes beautifully — beauty is not accuracy; accuracy wants quadrants, and knees willing to sit a night. On the road he lives on dates and thin bread; refusing tea is rude, refusing stellar counsel is thought suicidal. Carry him a stage, and he will give you the silk bag of dice and, in a Gobi night, point out Vega's blue: 'Dice are a shortcut; that blue is the master.' On the eastward road he rarely asks strangers for coin — only what color the stars were last night.",
    perkZh: "星骰仪式失败时，可免费重掷一次", perkEn: "One free re-roll whenever an astro-dice rite fails",
    biasZh: "宿命之眼", biasEn: "The fatalist eye"
  },
  lin: {
    ic: "⛵", civ: "mazu", zh: "船娘林三娘", en: "Lin Sanniang the Boatwoman",
    bioZh: "林三娘——刺桐船户人家的女儿，浪里生浪里长。父亲掌三桅福船，载过暹罗香木、爪哇丁香、波斯蓝靛；她自小跑南海，学了一口能跟各国水手讨价的混杂话。她说天妃听得懂所有口音的祷告——法郎机人、波斯人、占城人在天妃宫前伏地，女神从不偏袒任何一种语言。海上规矩比陆上公道：风暴来时，不问谁的儿子，只看谁的帆收得快。她穿短褐赤足近水，风大时布裹头；离港前必掷筊——圣筊方行，阴筊则止，笑筊重问，三次为限。胡椒在刺桐可抵宅院，她却说货再多，风一变全是纸。出海前不吃「翻」音之菜——忌讳比菜单更硬。她跟你的船，不是为了盘缠，是因为她听见你在天妃殿前的口音生疏却诚恳——『诚恳比银子沉。』预兆上她偏民俗之耳：庙签、筊杯、船工忌讳，她比星盘更信。若你问她法兰克人能不能活过印度洋，她只笑：『先问神，再开舱。』她账簿上不记银两，只记风向与掷筊结果——银两会骗你，风和神不会。若船上有人哭着问能不能活，她不给安慰话，只把筊杯塞进对方手里。",
    bioEn: "Lin Sanniang — daughter of a Zayton boat family, raised on the swells. Her father skippered a three-masted Fujian junk that carried Siamese fragrant wood, Javanese cloves, and Persian indigo; she grew up running the South Seas and learnt a mongrel tongue for haggling with any sailor afloat. Mazu, she says, understands every accent — Franks, Persians, Champa men prostrate before Tianfei, and the goddess never favors one tongue. The sea's rule is fairer than land's: when a storm rises, no one asks whose son you are — only how fast you strike sail. Near water she wears a short jacket and goes barefoot; in a gale she wraps her head. Before casting off she casts the moon-blocks: Sheng-jiao to sail, Yin to stop, Xiao to ask again — three casts the limit. Pepper at Zayton can buy a house; she still says cargo without end is paper when the wind turns. Before sailing she refuses dishes whose names sound like 'overturn' — taboo harder than any menu. She joins your ship not for coin, but because she heard your strange yet earnest accent before the Tianfei hall — 'Earnestness weighs more than silver.' In omens she trusts the folklorist's ear: temple lots, moon-blocks, sailors' taboos over any astrolabe. Ask whether a Frank can live through the Indian Ocean, and she only smiles: 'Ask the goddess first; open the hold second.' If someone on board weeps asking whether they will live, she offers no comfort — only presses the moon-blocks into their hands.",
    perkZh: "掷筊前三掷免耗，且预兆更准", perkEn: "First three jiaobei casts free, omens sharper",
    biasZh: "民俗之耳", biasEn: "The folklorist ear"
  }
};

